WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.410
A customer wants two deals to cost exactly
the same at five drinks.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.294
Keep the drink prices fixed.

00:00:06.294 --> 00:00:09.241
Your design choice is the joining fee for
club A.

00:00:10.171 --> 00:00:12.070
Start with the deal you already know.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.783
B costs two dollars to join and four per
drink.

00:00:15.193 --> 00:00:17.007
Five drinks add twenty dollars.

00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:19.183
With the fee, B costs twenty-two.

00:00:20.113 --> 00:00:22.423
Club A charges two dollars per drink.

00:00:22.423 --> 00:00:24.073
Five drinks add ten dollars.

00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:28.556
What fee would bring that ten-dollar order
up to twenty-two? Pause and guess.

00:00:30.936 --> 00:00:32.279
Subtract ten from twenty-two.

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:35.299
The joining fee for A should be twelve
dollars.

00:00:35.709 --> 00:00:39.772
On the graph, both lines now pass through
five drinks and twenty-two dollars.

00:00:40.702 --> 00:00:43.878
At four drinks, A costs twenty and B costs
eighteen.

00:00:43.878 --> 00:00:46.736
At six, A costs twenty-four and B costs
twenty-six.

00:00:47.146 --> 00:00:49.706
The cheaper deal really does switch sides
at five.

00:00:50.636 --> 00:00:54.348
Give the two clubs the same price per drink
but different joining fees.

00:00:54.758 --> 00:00:57.315
Now both totals climb by the same amount.

00:00:57.315 --> 00:00:58.914
That starting gap never closes.
